quick_facts: Building block of all ordinary matter
quick_facts: Over 99.9% of an atom's mass is packed into its nucleus
quick_facts: Electrons occupy probability clouds (orbitals), not fixed orbits
quick_facts: About 10 million atoms lined up span just 1 millimeter
quick_facts: Number of protons fixes which element it is

sources:
    {"organization":"Britannica","title":"Atom","url":"https://www.britannica.com/science/atom"}

definition:
    The smallest unit of ordinary matter that keeps the identity of a chemical element. Every atom has a tiny, dense nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons bound to it electromagnetically. Atoms are overwhelmingly empty space — nearly all the mass sits in the nucleus, yet the electron cloud spans roughly 100,000 times farther out.

debunking_snippet:
    “Atoms are tiny solid balls.” They're almost entirely empty space — the solid feel of matter comes from electromagnetic forces between electron clouds, not hard surfaces actually touching.
